#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_function

#% text
# `inverse_function(f)` gives the inverse of the function f.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Named elementary functions map to their inverses (`sin` to `asin`, `exp` to `log`, ...). For a function of one argument the equation f(y) == x is solved and the first solution, its arbitrary constants set to 0, becomes a function of x.

#% item
# Otherwise `inverse_function(f)` stays as it is.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_function(sin)
#% output
#= asin

#% cell
inverse_function(t => 2*t + 3)(x)
#% output
#= (1/2)*(-3 + x)

#% cell
inverse_function(t => tan(exp(t)))(x)
#% output
#= log(atan(x))

#% cell
inverse_function(t => (a*t + b)/(c*t + d))(x)
#% output
#= (-b + d*x)/(a - c*x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `solve`, `asin`, `log`
